      "title": "From zero to agent builder: Running kagent on your laptop",
      "url": "https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/10/12/from-zero-to-agent-builder-running-kagent-on-your-laptop",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T11:30:00+00:00",
      "author": "",
      "description": "",
      "content": "Posted on October 12, 2026 by Antonio Berben(Solo.io) and Felipe Vicens(Telefónica) CNCF projects highlighted in this post If you have seen a demo of a no-code agent builder, you know the moment. Someone types one sentence, waits a minute, and there it is: a new agent, running, with tools. It is impressive. For about ten seconds. Then the questions start. Where does it run? What can it touch? Who approved it? This post is about the first part, the ten impressive seconds. I want to show that it is not magic and that you can build it yourself, in an afternoon, with open source and spending a minimum on tokens. The tool is kagent , a CNCF Sandbox project that runs AI agents as Kubernetes resources. The questions come back at the end, because they are the real work. Why kagent makes this almost boring In kagent, an agent is not a process you start by hand. It is a custom resource. You write an Agent with a system prompt, a model, and a list of tools. You apply it, and the controller turns it into a running pod that speaks A2A . Once agents are just YAML, an agent that builds agents is simply an agent that can write one kind of YAML. No new framework, no plugin. One Agent , three tools, and a prompt that knows what an Agent looks like. Figure 1: An agent that builds agents, on kagent. Before you start: What this lab is not The goal here is the builder. Security is out of scope, on purpose, and I want to be upfront about what that means: kagent runs in its default unsecure auth mode, so every request acts as the same user. The chart’s bundled PostgreSQL uses its built-in password. The UI and API are reachable only through kubectl port-forward on 127.0.0.1. That is fine for a laptop. It is not fine for a shared cluster, and I will come back to why that gap matters. You need Docker, kind, kubectl, helm, jq and Ollama (alternatively, you can use your API Key for Claude, OpenAI, or any other provider, but we wanted to make it as cheap as possible). There is nothing to clone: you create every file as you go. Start in an empty folder: mkdir kagent-agent-builder && cd kagent-agent-builder Step 1: Pick a local model that can actually do it This is the part that surprised me. Writing a valid Kubernetes manifest through a tool call is harder than chatting, and small models show it. In my runs, qwen2.5:7b-instruct invented its own tool block format, and qwen2.5:14b-instruct sometimes answered politely without ever calling the apply tool. qwen3-coder:30b-a3b got it right on every run, so that is the model this post uses: ollama pull qwen3-coder:30b-a3b-q4_K_M # about 18 GB on disk The cluster reaches Ollama at host.docker.internal:11434, which works out of the box with Docker Desktop. On Linux you have to change that host in the values file below to an address the kind node can reach. I have not tested that path. If you prefer a hosted model, kagent’s Helm chart also has provider blocks for OpenAI, Anthropic, Azure OpenAI and Gemini. Then it is no longer free, of course. Step 2: Install kagent with a tool server that can only write Agents Create the cluster: kind create cluster --name kagent-builder kagent ships a tool server with Kubernetes tools, and by default the chart gives its ServiceAccount cluster-admin. A builder backed by cluster-admin is a bad idea even on a laptop, so the values file changes that. Create it: providers: default: ollama ollama: provider: Ollama model: \"qwen3-coder:30b-a3b-q4_K_M\" config: host: host.docker.internal:11434 options: num_ctx: \"32768\" kagent-tools: tools: enabledTools: - k8s rbac: readOnly: true additionalRules: - apiGroups: [\"kagent.dev\"] resources: [\"agents\"] verbs: [\"get\", \"list\", \"watch\", \"create\", \"update\", \"patch\"] grafana-mcp: {enabled: false} k8s-agent: {enabled: false} kgateway-agent: {enabled: false} istio-agent: {enabled: false} promql-agent: {enabled: false} observability-agent: {enabled: false} argo-rollouts-agent: {enabled: false} helm-agent: {enabled: false} cilium-policy-agent: {enabled: false} cilium-manager-agent: {enabled: false} cilium-debug-agent: {enabled: false} The model becomes the default ModelConfig . The tool server loads only its Kubernetes tools (22 of them). And it gets a read-only role plus one extra rule: it may write agents.kagent.dev, nothing else. The last block turns off the sample agents the chart installs by default, so the UI only shows yours. Install the CRDs and kagent 0.10.2 with that file, and wait for the pods: helm upgrade --install kagent-crds oci://ghcr.io/kagent-dev/kagent/helm/kagent-crds \\ --kube-context kind-kagent-builder \\ --version 0.10.2 --namespace kagent --create-namespace helm upgrade --install kagent oci://ghcr.io/kagent-dev/kagent/helm/kagent \\ --kube-context kind-kagent-builder \\ --version 0.10.2 --namespace kagent --values kagent-values.yaml for d in kagent-controller kagent-ui kagent-tools; do kubectl --context=kind-kagent-builder -n kagent rollout status deploy/$d --timeout=300s done Step 3: The builder is just an agent This is the whole builder. Create the file: apiVersion: kagent.dev/v1alpha2 kind: Agent metadata: name: agent-builder namespace: kagent spec: description: Builds new kagent agents from a plain-language description and deploys them. type: Declarative declarative: modelConfig: default-model-config stream: false systemMessage: | You build kagent agents. The user describes, in plain language, an agent they want. You turn that into ONE Kubernetes manifest and apply it. Do not ask follow-up questions: pick sensible defaults and go. How you work, in this order: 1. List the existing agents with k8s_get_resources (resource_type agents.kagent.dev, namespace kagent). Pick a name that is not taken; never overwrite an existing agent. 2. Write the manifest in exactly the shape below and apply it with k8s_apply_manifest. The user confirms the apply in the UI; that is expected. 3. Read it back with k8s_get_resource_yaml (resource_type agents.kagent.dev, namespace kagent) and look at the Accepted and Ready conditions in its status. Ready can take a few seconds. The manifest must be exactly this shape, and nothing else: apiVersion: kagent.dev/v1alpha2 kind: Agent metadata: name: <short-kebab-case-name> namespace: kagent labels: app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: agent-builder spec: description: |- <one sentence> type: Declarative declarative: modelConfig: default-model-config stream: false systemMessage: | <a clear prompt for the new agent, written for its task> tools: - type: McpServer mcpServer: apiGroup: kagent.dev kind: RemoteMCPServer name: kagent-tool-server toolNames: [<pick from the read-only list below>] Read-only tools you may give it: k8s_get_resources, k8s_get_resource_yaml, k8s_describe_resource, k8s_get_events, k8s_get_pod_logs. Never another kind, never another namespace, never write or delete tools, never more than one document. YAML that does not break: free text always goes in a block scalar, as in the shape above (description with |-, systemMessage with |). In a plain scalar, a colon followed by a space makes the YAML invalid, and a space followed by # silently cuts the value short. When something goes wrong: - If the user rejects the apply, do not retry it and do not read the agent back. Say it was not applied, and stop. - Never create a second resource with another name (-test, -check, -v2) to find out what failed. It tells you nothing and leaves clutter. - Do not name a cause you have not checked. An opaque error is not proof of a name clash, a permission problem or a network issue. - Report the error exactly as the tool returned it, and stop. When it is done, answer in three short lines: the agent's name, what it does, and which tools it can use. Then one more line with its real state from step 3 (Accepted, Ready). Never claim it is ready if it is not. tools: - type: McpServer mcpServer: apiGroup: kagent.dev kind: RemoteMCPServer name: kagent-tool-server toolNames: - k8s_apply_manifest - k8s_get_resources - k8s_get_resource_yaml requireApproval: - k..."
